valid mathematical principles. The solution uses reduction to vertical lines for non-sunny lines, counting arguments, integer lattice line representation, bounding number of points per line, case analysis, explicit construction for k=0,1,3. These are all valid methods. The solution also uses a bound derived from integer representation of lines and sum of coordinates. That is a valid method. The solution also uses a counting argument to derive inequality. All methods are valid.\n\nWe need to check if any step uses invalid reasoning. Let's examine each part.\n\n- Reduction to vertical lines: They claim we can replace each non-sunny line by a vertical line that passes through the leftmost column not yet occupied by a vertical line. They argue vertical lines cover all points of a column, while horizontal or slope -1 lines can meet a column in at most one point. This seems plausible: a vertical line x = a covers all points with that x-coordinate; a horizontal line y = b covers at most one point per column (since each column has at most one point with that y-coordinate). Similarly, a line of slope -1 (x+y = c) intersects each column at exactly one point (since for a given x, y = c - x). So indeed vertical lines are \"more efficient\" in covering points per column. So replacing a non-sunny line with a vertical line that covers at least as many points as the original line is valid. However, we need to ensure that the replacement does not cause duplication (i.e., two vertical lines with same x coordinate). They say \"passes through the leftmost column not yet occupied by a vertical line.\" So they ensure distinctness. So this reduction is valid.\n\n- Then they assume non-sunny lines are vertical lines x=1,...,x=n-k. This is valid after reduction.\n\n- Then they define T_k as points with x+y ≤ k+1 (since points with x ≤ n-k are covered, leaving points with x > n-k, i.e., x from n-k+1 to n? Actually they define T_k = { (x,y) ∈ Z_{>0}^2 | x+y ≤ k+1 }. Let's verify: original S_n